GLM-5.1 by @Zai_org just launched in the Text Arena and is now the #1 open model. It outperforms its predecessor, GLM-5, by 11 points and is 15 points ahead of Kimi K2.5 Thinking.

It shows strength as the #1 open model in Longer Query (#4 overall), Life, Physical & Social Sciences (#5 overall), Entertainment, Sports & Media (#8 overall), and Coding (#10 overall).

@Zai_org: "Introducing GLM-5.1: The Next Level of Open Source - Top-Tier Performance: #1 in open source and #3 globally across SWE-Bench Pro, Terminal-Bench, and NL2Repo. Built for Long-Horizon Tasks: Runs autonomously for 8 hours, refining strategies through thousands of iterations."

Based on @arena results, Opus 4.7 seems to be an incremental improvement upon Opus 4.6, although it is weaker in some categories. The big gain is in Code Arena performance for web development tasks.

Opus 4.7 and 4.6 currently have nearly identical scores in the overall leaderboard, but Opus 4.7 is slightly worse than 4.6 on expert prompts. It is still within the noise, but we will see where it shakes out. Notably, Opus 4.7 beats Opus 4.6 by over 30 points in Code Arena agentic web development, which is a significant gap. This highlights the importance of real human evaluations, which tell a story very different from the officially reported benchmarks.

@arena: "Exciting news - Claude Opus 4.7 from @AnthropicAI takes #1 in Code Arena! +37 points over Opus-4.6 and +46 over the next non-Anthropic model, GLM-5.1 (#4). Massive ~130 pts lead over GPT-5.4 and Gemini-3.1-Pro. #1 on both React and HTML leaderboards. Code Arena evaluates agentic coding on real-world tasks - building live websites and apps, ranked by users on real workflows. Huge congrats to @AnthropicAI on pushing the frontier forward again!"

Kimi-K2.6 is now live in the Arena, showing big improvements over Kimi-K2.5. It ranks as the #2 open model in Code Arena and #1 in both Vision and Document Arenas, with strong gains in agentic coding, vision, and long-context document tasks.

@Kimi_Moonshot: "Meet Kimi K2.6: Advancing Open-Source Coding. Open-source SOTA on various benchmarks, with new features like long-horizon coding, motion-rich frontend capabilities, elevated agent swarms, proactive agents, and Claw Groups for collaborative tasks. K2.6 is available in chat and agent modes for production-grade coding."

Kimi K2.6 enhances coding capabilities with features like 4,000+ tool calls and support for multiple programming languages, pushing the frontier for open models.

Exciting news - DeepSeek V4 Pro is in the Arena with 1.6 trillion parameters (49 billion activated) alongside V4 Flash at 284 billion parameters (13 billion activated). Both support a 1 million token context, marking a major leap over DeepSeek V3.2.

In the Code Arena, DeepSeek V4 Pro ranks as the #3 open model (#14 overall), on par with GPT-5.4-high and Gemini-3.1-Pro in agentic web development tasks. In the Text Arena, it ranks #2 open model (#14 overall), matching Kimi-2.6, while DeepSeek V4 Flash is #10 open model (#47 overall). The competition at the top of the open model leaderboards is heating up. Huge congrats to @DeepSeek_AI on the strong comeback.

@deepseek_ai: "🚀 DeepSeek-V4 Preview is officially live & open-sourced! Welcome to the era of cost-effective 1M context length. DeepSeek-V4-Pro: 1.6T total / 49B active params. Performance rivaling the world's top closed-source models. DeepSeek-V4-Flash: 284B total / 13B active params. Your fast, efficient, and economical choice. Try it now via Expert Mode / Instant Mode. API is updated & available today!"

Technically, the right approach would be Equal E.V. Scoring, which makes the points-per-shot depend on the shooter's location, incentivizing shooting from anywhere on the court. This means the next shot can come from anywhere.

Here's a diagram of the points-per-basket that would accomplish this with an expected points per shot of 2, no matter where you are on the court. It provides a rational reason for players to take half-court shots and beyond, which could be super cool.

@TheNBABase: "Steve Kerr says he would consider eliminating the three-point line to make the game more creative. I would never do a four-point play. In fact, I would even consider getting rid of the three-point line. I just think that the game, as it was designed, is really to create the best shots possible..."

Why is GPT-5.5 lower than Claude? The answer is simple: Code Arena currently only supports frontend/web development tasks, where GPT-5.5 is weakest. Full-stack app development and GitHub integration will be available in a couple of months.

Next time, we'll be clearer that this leaderboard shows React/FE only until we ship full-stack apps. Thanks for the feedback!

@arena: "GPT-5.5 by @OpenAI is now live in the Arena, landing across multiple leaderboards. Here’s how it ranks by modality: Code Arena (agentic web dev): #9, a strong +50pt jump over GPT-5.4; Document Arena (analysis & long-content reasoning): #6, on par with Sonnet 4.6; Text Arena: #7, Math #3, Instruction Following: #8; Expert Arena: #5; Search Arena: #2; Vision Arena: #5. Strong, well-rounded performance, especially in Code (+50 pts vs GPT-5.4). Congrats to @OpenAI on the release. Full category breakdowns by modality in the thread."

Giving agents wallets will be among the most important and consequential developments in history. It is the first step in disintermediating humans from capital allocation. The question arises: does capitalism still benefit humans when value assignment is done by agents with wallets?

@stripe: "Today, we’re launching the @link wallet for agents. It lets you securely empower agents to spend on your behalf. Your payment credentials are never exposed and you approve every purchase."

The linked service allows agents to pay online using single-use cards or automated payment protocols, with every request requiring your approval.

